WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:01.111
- Good morning, Believe Nation.

00:00:01.111 --> 00:00:02.381
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:02.381 --> 00:00:04.333
My one word is believe, and I believe

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:05.904
that entrepreneurs will solve all

00:00:05.904 --> 00:00:07.617
of the major problems of the world.

00:00:07.617 --> 00:00:10.751
So today's message is be patient.

00:00:10.751 --> 00:00:11.936
Over to you, Simon Sinek.

00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:14.646
(rooster crows)

00:00:14.646 --> 00:00:16.934
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:16.934 --> 00:00:19.620
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.120
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:24.408 --> 00:00:27.204
- I talk to so many smart, fantastic,

00:00:27.204 --> 00:00:30.537
ambitious, idealistic, hard-working kids,

00:00:30.537 --> 00:00:31.951
and they're right out of college.

00:00:31.951 --> 00:00:33.320
And they're in entry level jobs,

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.707
and I'll ask them, "How's it going?"

00:00:35.707 --> 00:00:38.787
And they'll say, "I
think I'm going to quit."

00:00:38.787 --> 00:00:40.584
And I'm like, why?

00:00:40.584 --> 00:00:43.687
And they say to me, "I'm
not making an impact."

00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:45.855
I'm like, you know you've
been here eight months, right?

00:00:45.855 --> 00:00:48.408
(audience laughs)

00:00:48.408 --> 00:00:51.898
They treat the sense of
fulfillment or even love

00:00:51.898 --> 00:00:53.542
like it's a scavenger hunt,

00:00:53.542 --> 00:00:55.244
like it's something you look for.

00:00:55.244 --> 00:00:58.262
My millennial friends, they've
gone through so many jobs.

00:00:58.262 --> 00:00:59.343
They're either getting fired.

00:00:59.343 --> 00:01:00.754
I mean, it was mutual.

00:01:00.754 --> 00:01:03.770
(audience laughs)

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.653
Or they're quitting because
they're not making an impact

00:01:06.653 --> 00:01:08.071
or they're not finding the
thing they're looking for.

00:01:08.071 --> 00:01:09.883
They're not feeling fulfilled as if

00:01:09.883 --> 00:01:11.244
it's a scavenger hunt.

00:01:11.244 --> 00:01:13.744
Love, a job you find joy from,

00:01:16.495 --> 00:01:18.352
is not something you discover.

00:01:18.352 --> 00:01:20.503
It's not like, I found love.

00:01:20.503 --> 00:01:21.542
Here it is.

00:01:21.542 --> 00:01:22.933
I found a job I love.

00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:24.676
That's not how it works.

00:01:24.676 --> 00:01:26.579
Both of those things require hard work.

00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:28.803
You are in love because you work very hard

00:01:28.803 --> 00:01:33.414
every single day of your
life to stay in love.

00:01:33.414 --> 00:01:35.968
You find a job that
brings you ultimate joy

00:01:35.968 --> 00:01:38.752
because you work hard every single day

00:01:38.752 --> 00:01:41.996
to serve those around you,
and you maintain that joy.

00:01:41.996 --> 00:01:44.014
It's not a discovery.

00:01:44.014 --> 00:01:46.575
But the problem is this
sense of impatience.

00:01:46.575 --> 00:01:48.923
It's as if an entire
generation is standing

00:01:48.923 --> 00:01:49.985
at the foot of a mountain.

00:01:49.985 --> 00:01:51.676
They know exactly what they want.

00:01:51.676 --> 00:01:52.653
They can see the summit.

00:01:52.653 --> 00:01:54.641
What they can't see is the mountain.

00:01:54.641 --> 00:01:57.058
This large, immovable object.

00:01:58.552 --> 00:02:00.190
That doesn't mean you
have to do your time.

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.678
That's not what I'm talking about.

00:02:01.678 --> 00:02:03.118
Take a helicopter, climb.

00:02:03.118 --> 00:02:04.156
I don't care.

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:05.887
But there's still a mountain.

00:02:05.887 --> 00:02:10.354
Life, career fulfillment,
relationships are journeys.

00:02:10.354 --> 00:02:13.180
The problem is this entire generation

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.879
has an institutionalized
sense of impatience.

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:17.333
And do they have the patience to go

00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:21.806
on the journey to maintain
love, to feel fulfilled,

00:02:21.806 --> 00:02:23.318
or do they just quit and on to the next.

00:02:23.318 --> 00:02:24.937
Dump and on to the next.

00:02:24.937 --> 00:02:27.283
Ghost and on to the next.

00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:28.902
- I'm a big fan of modeled success.

00:02:28.902 --> 00:02:31.907
I'm a believer that you
can shortcut your path

00:02:31.907 --> 00:02:35.219
to success by finding what
other people have done

00:02:35.219 --> 00:02:37.767
to achieve that success,
learning from them,

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:39.930
and applying it to your
own life, to your own path

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.635
to help you get there faster.

00:02:41.635 --> 00:02:43.267
I love finding shortcuts.

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:44.581
I love finding hacks.

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:46.917
I love finding all the
productivity tools and team

00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:49.726
and everything you can do to
climb that mountain faster.

00:02:49.726 --> 00:02:51.893
But to Simon's point,
there is still a mountain.

00:02:51.893 --> 00:02:54.363
There's still a lot of
work that has to go in.

00:02:54.363 --> 00:02:57.272
It's working smart and working hard.

00:02:57.272 --> 00:02:58.761
It's not just one or the other

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:00.505
to go and accomplish your goal.

00:03:00.505 --> 00:03:02.367
If you look at my YouTube
channel as an example,

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:04.858
since we're watching here all together,

00:03:04.858 --> 00:03:07.500
I've made 5,000 videos for this channel.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.310
4,000 of them are public, and
1,000 plus of them are not.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:12.210
They're practice videos.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.043
They're demo videos.

00:03:13.043 --> 00:03:14.342
They're test videos.

00:03:14.342 --> 00:03:16.591
I sucked at the start.

00:03:16.591 --> 00:03:20.145
Go back and watch my first
video, and look how bad it is.

00:03:20.145 --> 00:03:22.061
Somebody recently left a
comment on one of my videos

00:03:22.061 --> 00:03:24.528
I recorded five years ago, and she said,

00:03:24.528 --> 00:03:27.039
"Oh, my Evan, look at how far you've come.

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:28.933
"Five years later, you
sound and look different

00:03:28.933 --> 00:03:30.989
"in so many good ways."

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:33.584
And that's awesome to hear,
and also really frustrating.

00:03:33.584 --> 00:03:35.835
You know, I am mostly the same Evan

00:03:35.835 --> 00:03:37.184
that I was five years ago.

00:03:37.184 --> 00:03:39.270
My belief system, my values, you know,

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:40.729
maybe I've learned a little bit more.

00:03:40.729 --> 00:03:42.420
I've gained a little bit more experience.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.348
But why am I so much better now on camera

00:03:45.348 --> 00:03:47.765
than I was way back then?

00:03:47.765 --> 00:03:49.816
Well, it's because I practiced.

00:03:49.816 --> 00:03:52.033
What I had four, five years ago compared

00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:56.312
to what I have now, the
difference is 5,000 videos.

00:03:56.312 --> 00:03:58.718
You know, that 1,000 videos
that haven't been published,

00:03:58.718 --> 00:04:01.302
those trying and errors and failings

00:04:01.302 --> 00:04:04.521
on those 1,000 videos
that never got released

00:04:04.521 --> 00:04:07.971
are more than what most
people ever create.

00:04:07.971 --> 00:04:09.268
Most people who start a YouTube channel,

00:04:09.268 --> 00:04:11.933
they'll make three, four, five, 10 videos

00:04:11.933 --> 00:04:14.749
and quit 'cause it's too hard.

00:04:14.749 --> 00:04:15.878
'Cause it's too challenging.

00:04:15.878 --> 00:04:17.165
'Cause they don't want
to climb that mountain.

00:04:17.165 --> 00:04:19.157
'Cause they don't want to put in the work.

00:04:19.157 --> 00:04:22.198
Where here's 1,000 videos that
never saw the light of day

00:04:22.198 --> 00:04:23.963
and 4,000 more that are going.

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.658
Now, can you get there faster than me?

00:04:25.658 --> 00:04:26.780
Sure.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:27.732
You know, I'm looking at Alex.

00:04:27.732 --> 00:04:29.133
I'm lookin' at Lilly
who've been helping me

00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:30.268
on some of the videos.

00:04:30.268 --> 00:04:33.201
They are already better than when I was

00:04:33.201 --> 00:04:34.248
when I first started.

00:04:34.248 --> 00:04:36.657
They're already better than
I was maybe 1,000 videos in.

00:04:36.657 --> 00:04:38.455
You can get there faster than me.

00:04:38.455 --> 00:04:39.689
You can model success.

00:04:39.689 --> 00:04:42.176
Maybe you also have just
more natural talent.

00:04:42.176 --> 00:04:45.248
You can rise the ladder faster
than other people around you

00:04:45.248 --> 00:04:48.037
if you have more natural
talent, if you model success,

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:49.715
if you have a deep passion
for what you're doing.

00:04:49.715 --> 00:04:51.794
You can get there faster.

00:04:51.794 --> 00:04:53.880
But it's still a long road.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.274
You still have to be patient.

00:04:55.274 --> 00:04:57.180
You still have to put in the work,

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.373
and I think that's what people
often get too frustrated by

00:05:00.373 --> 00:05:02.919
is they want the immediate results.

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:04.542
They want the immediate success.

00:05:04.542 --> 00:05:08.485
And if they don't get it, then they quit.

00:05:08.485 --> 00:05:10.672
And so understand if this
is a deep passion of yours,

00:05:10.672 --> 00:05:12.124
if this is something
that you want to commit

00:05:12.124 --> 00:05:13.451
potentially the rest of your life to doing

00:05:13.451 --> 00:05:15.783
because you care about
it so much, then expect

00:05:15.783 --> 00:05:17.101
to suck at the start.

00:05:17.101 --> 00:05:19.726
Expect it to take a lot of time.

00:05:19.726 --> 00:05:22.406
Expect to work on trying
to improve your efficiency

00:05:22.406 --> 00:05:24.119
and productivity and skills and resources

00:05:24.119 --> 00:05:27.799
to get there fast as possible,
but expect to be committed.

00:05:27.799 --> 00:05:29.299
And stay patient.

00:05:29.299 --> 00:05:32.375
And if you are whether
it takes you 100 videos

00:05:32.375 --> 00:05:36.542
or 5,000 videos or more of the
equivalent in your industry,

00:05:38.093 --> 00:05:39.409
you'll get there.

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:40.846
So the question of the day today is,

00:05:40.846 --> 00:05:42.449
I'm curious about your reps.

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:43.282
I'm curious.

00:05:43.282 --> 00:05:45.084
How much are you putting
into your business?

00:05:45.084 --> 00:05:47.618
How much are you practicing
daily to get better

00:05:47.618 --> 00:05:50.229
at whatever skill it is you
have that you're bringing?

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:51.475
I'm curious about your schedule.

00:05:51.475 --> 00:05:53.209
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:05:53.209 --> 00:05:55.610
I'm excited to see what
you guys have to say.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.430
I also want to give a
quick shout-out to Laytina.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:59.864
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:05:59.864 --> 00:06:00.697
Your One Word.

00:06:00.697 --> 00:06:02.206
It really, really, means a lot to me,

00:06:02.206 --> 00:06:04.659
and I sincerely hope
you're enjoying the read.

00:06:04.659 --> 00:06:06.056
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:06:06.056 --> 00:06:07.340
I believe in you.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.243
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:06:09.243 --> 00:06:11.303
and whatever your one word is.

00:06:11.303 --> 00:06:12.784
And I'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:06:12.784 --> 00:06:15.280
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.890
♫ I wake up every morning

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:20.596
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:06:20.596 --> 00:06:22.048
♫ I wake up every morning

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:24.047
(typewriter clicks)

00:06:24.047 --> 00:06:27.474
- So a bunch, a group of
really brilliant psychologists

00:06:27.474 --> 00:06:30.778
in the field of expertise
research have sat down

00:06:30.778 --> 00:06:33.766
and tried to figure out how
long do you have to work

00:06:33.766 --> 00:06:37.014
at something before you
become really good, right?

00:06:37.014 --> 00:06:38.880
And the answer seems to
be, it's an extraordinary

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.118
consistent answer in an
incredible number of fields,

00:06:42.118 --> 00:06:43.954
and that is you need to have practiced,

00:06:43.954 --> 00:06:48.165
to have apprenticed for 10,000
hours before you get good.

00:06:48.165 --> 00:06:51.543
So every great classical
composer without exception

00:06:51.543 --> 00:06:53.122
composes for at least 10 years

00:06:53.122 --> 00:06:54.466
before they write their master work.

00:06:54.466 --> 00:06:55.466
- Mozart 10?

00:06:56.323 --> 00:06:57.637
- Mozart is composing at 11,

00:06:57.637 --> 00:06:59.935
but he's composing garbage at 11.

00:06:59.935 --> 00:07:03.321
I mean, he doesn't produce
something great until 22 or 23,

00:07:03.321 --> 00:07:05.755
Concerto number nine, I think, to 71.

00:07:05.755 --> 00:07:07.867
If I asked you how long did it take you

00:07:07.867 --> 00:07:10.105
when you were doing this job
before you were comfortable

00:07:10.105 --> 00:07:11.398
and good and what you were doing?

00:07:11.398 --> 00:07:12.300
- 10 years at least.
- Yeah, 10 years.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.176
- Yeah.

00:07:13.176 --> 00:07:14.050
- And the same way with me.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.447
I mean, it's an incredibly
consistent finding,

00:07:15.447 --> 00:07:17.164
and it's really important because it says

00:07:17.164 --> 00:07:20.157
that we are far too impatient with people

00:07:20.157 --> 00:07:23.459
when we assess whether
someone has got what it takes

00:07:23.459 --> 00:07:24.474
to do a certain job.

00:07:24.474 --> 00:07:26.810
We always want to make that
assessment after six months

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:29.799
or a year or, and that's
ridiculous, you know.

00:07:29.799 --> 00:07:32.104
The kinds of jobs we have people do today

00:07:32.104 --> 00:07:33.753
are sufficiently complex.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:36.529
They require a long time to reach mastery.

00:07:36.529 --> 00:07:38.963
What we should be doing
is setting up institutions

00:07:38.963 --> 00:07:41.921
and structures that allow
people to spend the time

00:07:41.921 --> 00:07:45.084
and effort to reach master,
not judging them prematurely.

00:07:45.084 --> 00:07:47.903
- Patience is for the impatient.

00:07:47.903 --> 00:07:50.815
That, in fact, when
you're getting started,

00:07:50.815 --> 00:07:53.125
and your in-laws are making faces at you

00:07:53.125 --> 00:07:54.753
and you're not sure if
you're going to be able

00:07:54.753 --> 00:07:56.007
to pay the rent and you don't know

00:07:56.007 --> 00:07:57.542
why you're living in
Brooklyn to begin with.

00:07:57.542 --> 00:07:59.142
You're going to have to
move back to Florida.

00:07:59.142 --> 00:08:02.430
It's easy to say, you know what?

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:05.962
I need to go faster to
pick up these scraps,

00:08:05.962 --> 00:08:07.373
to pick up these scraps.

00:08:07.373 --> 00:08:10.001
And sometimes what that
does is it gives you

00:08:10.001 --> 00:08:11.714
the foot up to get to the next level.

00:08:11.714 --> 00:08:13.736
But sometimes what that does is

00:08:13.736 --> 00:08:15.998
it just makes you a scrap collector.

00:08:15.998 --> 00:08:18.227
And one of the things that we see

00:08:18.227 --> 00:08:21.052
when we look at the work
of people who have put

00:08:21.052 --> 00:08:23.168
really big ideas into the world,

00:08:23.168 --> 00:08:26.055
who have built online platforms, right?

00:08:26.055 --> 00:08:30.222
It's that they got there by
being patiently impatient

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:33.054
or impatiently patient, whichever way

00:08:33.054 --> 00:08:34.502
you want to juxtapose it.

00:08:34.502 --> 00:08:36.927
If you look around at the blogs you read

00:08:36.927 --> 00:08:40.257
or the people you respect
online or the organizations

00:08:40.257 --> 00:08:43.862
you want to work with, the
myth of the overnight success

00:08:43.862 --> 00:08:46.870
is just that, a myth, that,
you know, the much vaunted

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:51.513
Twitter was a failure, a
complete failure for two years.

00:08:51.513 --> 00:08:55.266
Nobody used it, and if
they took the mindset of

00:08:55.266 --> 00:08:56.776
well, if it doesn't work
in two weeks, we got to go

00:08:56.776 --> 00:08:59.699
go do something else, you
never would have heard of it.

00:08:59.699 --> 00:09:01.676
- You know, China's a very special place,

00:09:01.676 --> 00:09:04.725
and if you look at the big tech companies,

00:09:04.725 --> 00:09:07.256
Apple's done very well.

00:09:07.256 --> 00:09:09.263
Microsoft hasn't lost too much.

00:09:09.263 --> 00:09:11.389
And Google's, you know, lost a lot.

00:09:11.389 --> 00:09:13.518
So it's quite a bit of variation despite

00:09:13.518 --> 00:09:15.278
all three of those being talented,

00:09:15.278 --> 00:09:17.333
long-term oriented companies.

00:09:17.333 --> 00:09:19.943
And when you look at Apple,
one of the amazing things

00:09:19.943 --> 00:09:21.742
is when they did the iPhone.

00:09:21.742 --> 00:09:25.431
They negotiated and talked
and built relationships

00:09:25.431 --> 00:09:27.393
for six years before the iPhone

00:09:27.393 --> 00:09:31.309
was allowed to come to
China so from 2005 to 2011.

00:09:31.309 --> 00:09:33.222
And that took a lot of patience.

00:09:33.222 --> 00:09:35.969
And now, that's one of
their largest markets

00:09:35.969 --> 00:09:37.325
in the world for the iPhone.

00:09:37.325 --> 00:09:39.629
And so what we take away from that

00:09:39.629 --> 00:09:41.057
is a sense of great patience.

00:09:41.057 --> 00:09:43.808
So it may be soon that we
have a license in China,

00:09:43.808 --> 00:09:45.274
or it may take a couple of years.

00:09:45.274 --> 00:09:46.731
But we're going to be very patient,

00:09:46.731 --> 00:09:49.239
and we're looking forward
to a time, you know,

00:09:49.239 --> 00:09:51.300
a decade or two decades from now

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:54.005
when the Chinese middle class will want

00:09:54.005 --> 00:09:56.586
and embrace the kind of
content that we have.

